            "<strong>Definition and purpose.</strong> Vital signs are the measurements of the body's basic functions — temperature, pulse, respiration, blood pressure, oxygen saturation, with pain and level of consciousness now recorded alongside them — taken to establish a baseline, to detect change, to monitor the response to treatment and to trigger escalation. They are the most frequently performed procedure in nursing and the most frequently falsified: not by dishonesty, but by an estimated respiratory rate, a cuff of the wrong size, a thermometer read before it beeped, a saturation taken through nail varnish and a pain score never asked. Almost every patient who arrests on a general ward had abnormal observations in the preceding hours that were either not taken, not charted, not scored or not acted on — which is why current practice ties the observations to a <strong>scoring chart</strong> with a built-in escalation rule.",
